# Media Query Syntax 📝

A media query consists of a media type and one or more conditions (media features):

```css_not
@media screen and (min-width: 768px) {
  /* CSS rules here only apply if viewport is 768px or wider! */
  .sidebar {
    display: block;
  }
}
```

## Media Types:
- `all`: Suitable for all devices (default).
- `screen`: Computer screens, tablets, smartphones.
- `print`: Print preview mode and printer output.

# Modern Media Query Range Syntax 🚀

Modern CSS supports mathematical comparison operators (`>=`, `<=`, `<`, `>`), which are far more intuitive than legacy `min-width`/`max-width`:

```css_not
/* Traditional Syntax */
@media (min-width: 768px) and (max-width: 1024px) {
  .nav { padding: 12px; }
}

/* Modern Range Syntax (Clean & Intuitive!) */
@media (768px <= width <= 1024px) {
  .nav { padding: 12px; }
}

@media (width >= 1200px) {
  .container { max-width: 1140px; }
}
```

# Mobile-First vs Desktop-First 🥇

## 1. Mobile-First (Industry Best Practice):
Write base styles for small mobile screens first. Use `min-width` queries to progressively add complexity as screens grow wider:

```css_not
/* Base: Single column mobile layout */
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr;
}

/* Tablet (768px+) */
@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
}

/* Desktop (1024px+) */
@media (min-width: 1024px) {
  .grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); }
}
```

Mobile devices have less CPU power; mobile-first ensures they don't have to evaluate and override heavy desktop CSS rules!

# Responsive Navigation: Hamburger to Desktop Bar 🍔

```html_not
<nav class="navbar">
  <button class="hamburger-btn">☰</button>
  <ul class="nav-links">
    <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
    <li><a href="/courses">Courses</a></li>
    <li><a href="/about">About</a></li>
  </ul>
</nav>
```

```css_not
/* Mobile default: Hide desktop links, show hamburger */
.hamburger-btn { display: block; }
.nav-links { display: none; }

/* Desktop screen (768px+) */
@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .hamburger-btn { display: none; }
  .nav-links {
    display: flex;
    gap: 24px;
    list-style: none;
  }
}
```

# Dark Mode with `prefers-color-scheme` 🌗

Detect whether the user has Dark Mode enabled in their operating system settings:

```css_not
:root {
  --bg-color: #ffffff;
  --text-color: #0f172a;
  --card-bg: #f8fafc;
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root {
    --bg-color: #0f172a;
    --text-color: #f8fafc;
    --card-bg: #1e293b;
  }
}

body {
  background-color: var(--bg-color);
  color: var(--text-color);
}
```

# Orientation: Portrait vs Landscape 🔄

Detect whether device viewport height exceeds its width:

```css_not
@media (orientation: landscape) {
  /* Applies when phone is turned sideways or on wide desktop */
  .hero-banner {
    min-height: 50vh;
  }
}

@media (orientation: portrait) {
  .hero-banner {
    min-height: 100vh;
  }
}
```

# Print Stylesheets (`@media print`) 🖨️

Format web pages for physical printing or PDF exports:

```css_not
@media print {
  /* Hide interactive elements not needed on paper */
  nav, footer, .sidebar, .ads, button {
    display: none !important;
  }

  body {
    color: #000000;
    background: #ffffff;
    font-size: 12pt;
  }

  /* Display URL next to links so readers know where links point */
  a[href]::after {
    content: " (" attr(href) ")";
  }
}
```

# The Revolution: Container Queries (`@container`) 📦

Traditional media queries check the **browser viewport**. But what if a component sits in a narrow sidebar on desktop? Viewport queries fail because the screen is wide, but the component's parent container is narrow!

**Container Queries** allow a component to style itself based on the width of its **parent container**:

```css_not
/* 1. Define container context on the parent */
.sidebar-wrapper, .main-wrapper {
  container-type: inline-size;
  container-name: card-container;
}

/* 2. Style the card based on container width! */
@container card-container (min-width: 400px) {
  .card {
    display: flex; /* Horizontal layout when container has room! */
  }
}
```

# Container Query Units (`cqw`, `cqh`) 📏

Just like `vw` represents 1% of the viewport width, Container Query units represent percentages of the parent container:

- `1cqw`: 1% of container's inline width.
- `1cqh`: 1% of container's height.

```css_not
.widget-title {
  /* Scales typography based on parent widget width, not full screen! */
  font-size: clamp(1rem, 5cqw, 2rem);
}
```

# Responsive Images & Aspect Ratio 📐

Prevent images and videos from ever breaking page boundaries:

```css_not
img, video {
  max-width: 100%;
  height: auto;
  display: block;
}

/* Maintain modern 16:9 aspect ratio containers */
.video-wrapper {
  aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
  width: 100%;
}
```

# Feature Queries with `@supports` 🕵️

Detect if a user's browser supports modern CSS features before applying them:

```css_not
/* Fallback for older browsers */
.gallery {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}

/* Progressive enhancement for modern browsers */
@supports (display: grid) {
  .gallery {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  }
}
```

# Real-World: Full Responsive Hero Section 📱💻

```html_not
<section class="hero">
  <div class="hero-content">
    <h1>Supercharge Your Code</h1>
    <p>The interactive platform for web developers.</p>
    <div class="actions">
      <button class="btn primary">Start Free</button>
      <button class="btn secondary">View Demo</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
```

```css_not
.hero {
  padding: 40px 20px;
  text-align: center;
}

.actions {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column; /* Stacked buttons on mobile */
  gap: 12px;
  margin-top: 24px;
}

/* Tablet & Desktop */
@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .hero {
    padding: 100px 40px;
  }
  .hero h1 {
    font-size: 3.5rem;
  }
  .actions {
    flex-direction: row; /* Side-by-side buttons on desktop */
    justify-content: center;
  }
}
```
